title: Every anomaly in one blob

steps:
  - text: |
      anomalies.pb carries one example of every annotation prototext can
      emit. Read top to bottom it goes from bytes every parser accepts,
      through bytes that disagree with the schema, to bytes no parser can
      make sense of at all.

      Each example sits in a submessage of its own, and where an anomaly
      has an ordinary counterpart the two are written next to each other.

      Press space to hand navigation to this pane, then ^-> to step.
      Press w at any time to show the wire bytes under each line.

  - text: |
      1. Legal, not canonical.

      A varint may carry leading padding, and a field tag is a varint.
      This tag is written in three bytes where one would do. The line
      under it says the same thing with the tag every writer emits.
    fold: all
    unfold: [/1]
    node: /1/2
    wire-lines:
      from: /1/2
      to: /1/3

  - text: |
      A length prefix is a varint too, and can be padded the same way.
      The four bytes of length below describe a string that needs one —
      and again the next line is the same string, prefixed canonically.
    fold: all
    unfold: [/2]
    node: /2/2
    wire-lines:
      from: /2/2
      to: /2/3

  - text: |
      And so can a value. `number: 5` is one byte of information written
      in four. The second field carries the same 5 in one.
    fold: all
    unfold: [/3, /3/2, /3/3]
    node: /3/2/2
    wire-line: /3/2/2

  - text: |
      2. Values that survive a round trip but not a re-encode.

      -1 as an int32 is specified to occupy ten bytes. This producer
      wrote five. It reads back as -1 everywhere — and re-encoding it
      gives different bytes than it arrived as: the ten below.
    fold: all
    unfold: [/4, /4/2, /4/3]
    node: /4/2/2
    wire-lines:
      from: /4/2/2
      to: /4/3/2

  - text: |
      There is one canonical NaN and 2^52 - 1 other ones. This is one of
      the others. Every arithmetic unit agrees it is NaN; no two of them
      agree on which — and the option below it, which reads identically,
      is the one a re-encode would write.
    fold: all
    unfold: [/5, /5/1, /5/2]
    node: /5/1/2
    wire-lines:
      from: /5/1/2
      to: /5/2/2

  - text: |
      3. A newer producer against an older schema.

      The wire carries enum value 99. The schema this session was opened
      with has no name for it, so the number is all there is to show —
      unlike the value below, which it does have a name for.
    fold: all
    unfold: [/6, /6/2, /6/3]
    node: /6/2/2

  - text: |
      The same disagreement, one step further: four fields the schema
      does not declare at all. Nothing is lost — the wire type is in the
      tag, so each one is still read, named by shape rather than by name.
    fold: all
    unfold: [/7]
    node: /7/2
    wire-lines:
      from: /7/2
      to: /7/5

  - text: |
      4. A packed run.

      Three text lines. One wire record. The w row is what makes that
      visible: the bytes belong to the run, not to any one line of it.
      Under it is a second run holding the same three numbers, written
      without the padding — one run against the other.
    fold: all
    unfold: [/8, /8/1]
    node: /8/1/2
    wire-node: /8/1/2

  - text: |
      5. The blob and the schema disagree.

      The schema says field 1 here is a string. The tag says varint. The
      tag wins — it is what the bytes actually are — and the field is
      shown by wire type instead.
    fold: all
    unfold: [/9]
    node: /9/2

  - text: |
      Declared a string, and it is a LEN record, so the shape is right.
      The payload is not valid UTF-8. The bytes are shown as they are.
    fold: all
    unfold: [/10]
    node: /10/2
    wire-line: /10/2

  - text: |
      Declared a packed int32 field, and the payload does not decode as
      one. The record is kept whole rather than guessed at.
    fold: all
    unfold: [/11]
    node: /11/1/2
    wire-line: /11/1/2

  - text: |
      6. Malformed wire bytes.

      From here on the bytes are not merely unexpected, they are
      unreadable — and each one takes the rest of its enclosing region
      with it. That is why every one of them sits alone in its own
      submessage: the length prefix is what bounds the damage.

      A length prefix claiming more bytes than the record has.
    fold: all
    unfold: [/12]
    node: /12/2
    wire-line: /12/2

  - text: |
      A varint with no terminating byte. There is no way to know where
      the next tag would have started.
    fold: all
    unfold: [/13]
    node: /13/2
    wire-line: /13/2

  - text: |
      A length prefix that is itself an unterminated varint, a 64-bit
      field with three bytes behind it, and a 32-bit field with two.
      Different fields, one failure: the record runs off its own end.
    fold: all
    unfold: [/14, /15, /16]
    node: /14/2

  - text: |
      Wire type 6 does not exist. Field number 0 is out of range. Both
      are tags that cannot be interpreted at all, which means nothing
      after them can be found either.
    fold: all
    unfold: [/17, /18]
    node: /17/2

  - text: |
      7. Groups.

      A group is opened by a tag and closed by a tag, with no length
      prefix anywhere. Nothing in this schema declares one, so all of
      these arrive as undeclared fields.
    fold: all
    unfold: [/19, /19/2]
    node: /19/2
    wire-node: /19/2

  - text: |
      Below it, the same group again, whose closing tag is padded — the
      tag_ohb of the first step, on the other end of the group.
    fold: all
    unfold: [/19, /19/3]
    node: /19/3
    wire-node: /19/3

  - text: |
      Opened as field 102 and closed as field 103; closed with a field
      number no field may have; and never closed at all. A group's two
      ends have to agree, and nothing on the wire enforces it.
    fold: all
    unfold: [/20, /21, /22]
    node: /20/2

  - text: |
      And last, an END_GROUP tag closing a group that was never opened.

      That is the whole vocabulary. Press space to take navigation back
      and explore on your own — the script does not constrain anything,
      so stepping back to any point picks up exactly where it left off.
    fold: all
    unfold: [/23]
    node: /23/2
    wire-line: /23/2
